# 3. 🎯 Don't Treat All Tasks Equally

This is probably the most important practical lesson.

Imagine your daily to-do list:

```text
Reply to messages
Check email
Watch tutorials
Study DSA
Work on project
Attend class
Organize files
Scroll Instagram
Practice interview questions
```

They don't all have equal value.

Some activities produce almost no meaningful progress.

Others can dramatically change your results.

### 80/20 thinking asks:

**Which tasks actually move my life forward?**

For your placement preparation, for example:

```text
100 hours of preparation
        ↓
Identify high-value activities
        ↓
DSA patterns
Core CS
Projects
Aptitude
Interview communication
        ↓
Reduce low-value activities
```

The objective isn't to study less blindly.

It's to **allocate more effort toward high-impact activities**.

---

# 4. 🧠 80/20 + Your DSA Preparation

This principle is extremely useful for DSA.

Suppose you have hundreds of LeetCode problems.

Trying to solve everything isn't necessarily the best strategy.

Instead, identify recurring patterns:

* Hashing
* Two pointers
* Sliding window
* Binary search
* Stack
* Linked list
* Trees
* Graphs
* Greedy
* Dynamic programming

Then identify the patterns that repeatedly appear in your target assessments.

### Instead of:

> "I need to solve 500 problems."

Think:

> **"Which patterns will give me the greatest improvement per hour?"**

That's 80/20 thinking.

